WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.400
RYAN MATSUMOTO: Cloud
Bigtable is a NoSQL database

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.890
that has the scale,
speed, and reliability

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.080
necessary to handle
big data workloads.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.275
In previous episodes,
we learned about some

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:12.750
of Bigtable's key features,
as well as its data model.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.250
We also took a look
at how Bigtable

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.560
is being used in a diverse
group of industries,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.500
like retail, transportation
and fraud detection.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.690
In this episode, we're
going to learn more about

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.290
how Bigtable can be used for a
key use case, personalization.

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:34.650
Across industries,
data is helping

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.220
businesses elevate the customer
experience to new heights.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.170
One particular area
where data is helping

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.350
is personalization, delivering
a tailored experience

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.780
to each customer based
on their own unique needs

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.950
and preferences.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.350
Personalization
is all around us.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.180
If you're an online
shopper, you might

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.310
notice the product
recommendations that pop up

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.440
based on your purchase history.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.282
If you use a video
streaming service,

00:00:57.282 --> 00:00:58.740
you'll probably
get recommendations

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.020
for new movies and TV
shows based on what

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.860
you've watched in the past.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.670
And if you like music, your
digital music service probably

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.130
uses personalization to
help you discover new songs

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:08.970
and playlists.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.010
Data is at the heart
of personalization,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.780
but this presents
unique challenges

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.850
to businesses trying
to harness it.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.850
Luckily, Bigtable is great
for supporting personalization

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.510
at scale because of its unique
combination of scale, speed,

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.280
and reliability.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.860
Personalization requires
businesses to store and access

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.090
large volumes of data.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.060
Bigtable is a highly
scalable database,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.580
allowing you to store
up to petabytes of data.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.250
In fact, Bigtable
supports trillions of rows

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.870
with millions of columns.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.670
And each row in
Bigtable supports up

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.820
to 256 megabytes of
data, so you can easily

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.760
store all personalization data
for an individual customer

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.830
in a single row.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.520
Bigtable databases can be
scaled linearly at zero downtime

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.340
by adding or removing
nodes, each of which

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.100
provides up to 10,000
additional queries per second

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.040
and is attached to
additional storage capacity.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.050
Personalization also
requires a database

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.750
that can quickly write large
volumes of data concurrently

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.330
for many users, since
customer behavior needs

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.380
to be continually captured to
inform future recommendations.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.360
Bigtable meets this
challenge with the ability

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.050
to handle millions of read
and write requests per second.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.730
And it's able to do
so at low latency,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.140
on the order of
single-digit milliseconds.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.828
This is critical
for personalization,

00:02:21.828 --> 00:02:23.370
since you wouldn't
want users to have

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.460
to wait around for
personalized recommendations.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.830
Bigtable's flexible
NoSQL data model also

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.960
works well for personalization.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.930
Traditional relational
databases assume a fixed schema

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.750
that will change
infrequently over time.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.930
But NoSQL databases relax
the rigidity of the schema

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.180
and allow for data structures
to evolve much more

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.170
easily over time.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.350
Another important aspect
of the Bigtable data model

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.710
is that its tables are sparse,
meaning that if a column is not

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.550
built in for a given row, you
don't pay for that empty cell.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.500
In an e-commerce
example, you might

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.930
have a row for each customer
and a column for each product

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.550
that they may have engaged with.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.770
With Bigtable's
NoSQL data model,

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.780
it's easy to add new
columns to the table

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.610
as new products are introduced.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.400
And if a customer hasn't
engaged with a given product,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.050
that cell will be
empty, and you don't

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.510
end up paying for its space,
because Bigtable tables are

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.220
sparse.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.560
Of course, storing
customer information

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.250
is only one step to performing
personalization at scale.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.830
The next step is finding
ways to make that data useful

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.920
using machine learning and
big data analysis tools.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.110
Bigtable has you covered
with easy integration

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:31.710
with Google Tools like
BigQuery, TensorFlow, Dataproc,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.210
and Dataflow, as well as Apache
tools like HBase, Hadoop, Beam,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.740
and Spark.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.720
We've covered just a
few of the key reasons

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.180
why Bigtable is great
for personalization.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.700
But other reasons include
low Total Cost of Ownership,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.330
or TCO, for high
throughput workloads;

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.640
consistent performance around
the world because of data

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.910
replication across Google
data centers internationally;

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.860
and business-critical
reliability,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:01.620
with up to 99.999%
availability SLA.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.420
Let's take a look at an example
of how Bigtable tables could

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.840
be used for an e-commerce
website looking to recommend

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.600
products to users based on their
viewing and purchase history.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.050
First, we might have
a user history table.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.402
This table stores information
about user sessions,

00:04:15.402 --> 00:04:16.860
including which
products they view,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.560
add to their cart, and purchase.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.560
Next, we could have a user
personalization table.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.570
This table stores
recommended products

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.630
for each user, which would be
generated dynamically over time

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.350
using machine learning.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.100
It can also store users' own
stated preferences from a wish

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.420
list of saved items.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.560
And finally, we might have a
product recommendation table.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.480
This would provide
product recommendations

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.440
based on an original product.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.980
For example, if a customer
bought a blue t-shirt,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.340
we might recommend them
other types of blue t-shirts.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.020
So there you have
it, an overview

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.410
of how Bigtable's speed,
scalability, and reliability

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.350
makes it great for
personalization use cases.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.720
If you're curious to learn
more, follow the links

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.250
below for more information.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.750
Thanks for joining
us today and stay

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.560
tuned for more episodes
about Bigtable.

